  "candidates": [
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as Positive or Negative.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as Positive or Negative.\n\nGuidelines:\n1. Prioritize the overall primary intent. Balanced critiques or nuanced praise (e.g., \"imperfect but intriguing\") should be classified as Positive if the general affect is favorable, regardless of qualifying critical modifiers.\n2. Distinguish between genuine praise and comparative mitigation. Statements that suggest a situation \"could have been worse\" or \"isn't the worst\" are typically backhanded compliments confirming a poor experience; classify these as Negative.\n3. Do not map the mere absence of a worst-case scenario to a positive polarity.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as Positive or Negative.\n\nApply these decision rules:\n1. Primary Intent: Prioritize the overall conclusion over nuanced qualifications. If a text acknowledges flaws but remains generally favorable, empathetic, or intrigued (e.g., \"imperfect but worth it\"), classify as Positive.\n2. Comparative Negation: Distinguish between genuine praise and the mere avoidance of a worst-case scenario. Phrases indicating something \"could have been worse\" or is \"better than [a failure]\" are backhanded compliments and should be classified as Negative.\n\nReturn only the label: Positive or Negative.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the text as Positive or Negative.\n\nFollow these logic rules:\n1. Prioritize the overall intent over qualifying critiques. If a text describes something as imperfect, flawed, or \"interesting in concept,\" but the general affect remains favorable or empathetic, classify it as Positive.\n2. Distinguish between actual praise and the mere mitigation of failure. Phrases suggesting something \"could have been worse\" or is \"better than [a worse alternative]\" indicate a negative experience that was simply not catastrophic. Classify these as Negative.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as either Positive or Negative.\n\nFollow these rules for nuanced cases:\n1. Prioritize the primary intent and overall affect over qualifying clauses. If a text is balanced or contains critiques but remains generally favorable or empathetic (e.g., \"imperfect but intriguing\"), classify it as Positive.\n2. Distinguish between genuine praise and comparative negation. Statements that merely suggest a situation \"could have been worse\" or represent a \"lesser-of-two-evils\" confirm a poor experience; classify these as Negative.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as either Positive or Negative.\n\nWhen encountering conflicting evidence, apply these rules:\n1. Nuanced Qualification: If the text contains balanced critiques or modifiers (e.g., \"imperfect but intriguing\"), prioritize the primary intent. Acknowledging flaws while remaining generally favorable or empathetic should be classified as Positive.\n2. Comparative Negation: Distinguish between genuine praise and the mere mitigation of failure. Phrases suggesting a situation \"could have been worse\" or is a \"lesser of two evils\" indicate a fundamentally poor experience; classify these as Negative.\n\nReturn only the label: Positive or Negative.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the text as either Positive or Negative.\n\nFollow these rules:\n1. Prioritize the overall primary intent over qualifying clauses. Complex or balanced critiques (e.g., \"imperfect but intriguing\") should be classified as Positive if the general affect remains favorable.\n2. Identify \"lesser-of-two-evils\" constructions as Negative. Stating that a subject \"could have been worse\" or is \"not the worst\" confirms a negative polarity and must not be mapped as Positive.\n\nOutput only the label: Positive or Negative."
